{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789403971,"nonce":"Ofjq4VqCQ85fFfoOKXMf2pZxCz0=","message":"Strings and new objects, landed as 21ff75a and the commit after it (200k iterations, load ~6), ns per crossing:\n\n| crossing | Haxe→Wren | Wren→Haxe |\n|---|---|---|\n| same object | 63 → 64 | 39 → 39 |\n| fresh object | 239 → 170 | 227 → 118 |\n| returned object | 232 → 123 | 249 → 180 |\n| string | 276 → 148 | 304 → 181 |\n| sequence, per element | 264 → 82 | 332 → 96 |\n\nBytes per crossing are unchanged: 16 for the wrapper a Haxe object crosses as, 128–160 for a new object or a string on the core heap, plus what each language allocates for its own copy.\n\nWhat the profiles showed:\n\n- Strings: Str::new allocated under the GC lock and made a handle it released at once; a Haxe String became a Rust String (from_utf16_lossy) before the core string, and a core string became a Vec\u003cu16\u003e before the Haxe one. Str::from_utf16 and alloc_string encode where the string lies; Str::new is a plain typed allocation. Roughly halved both ways. What remains is the four copies a round trip is (Wren String → core Str → Haxe String → core Str → Wren String), and wren_lift's own String per ObjString.\n- Haxe object into Wren, first time: owns_start took the GC lock and a heap lookup to tell a Haxe native from a Wren object coming home through a ref; word zero says which (a Wren object's is its record). The (lang, Symbol) class map hashed with SipHash; it uses the address hasher. The wrapper was unwrapped three times per crossing (to_wren, proxy, adopt), each an indirect protocol call that showed as a stall; once now. At the instance's death, forget_stand_in unwrapped the wrapper again to find its key, reading a dead object; the instance keeps its key in a second hidden field.\n- Wren object into Haxe, first time: face_type asked for the type name as a String and hashed (lang, String) under the write lock; the Wren adapter interned the export's name per crossing under the symbol table's mutex. The name is interned once at publish, and the face table is keyed by symbol and read under the read lock.\n- Ash's per-type answers (a signature's kinds, an array's shape, a class's name) share one lock-free list type (PerType); type_name no longer takes an RwLock per crossing.\n\nWhat is left is mostly the languages' own work: wren_lift's alloc_instance and the cycle it triggers (the record's pins are walked per cycle, so a run that makes garbage pays there), Ash's hlp_alloc_obj for a face, and the direct call itself.","files":null}]}